  "summary": "Healthcare-AI payment models are undergoing significant regulatory revision, with CMS proposing major changes to how it reimburses remote patient monitoring and clinical AI tools, while also attempting to ban third-party RPM providers and seeking to establish standardized payment structures tied to patient outcomes rather than pure technology deployment. The biotech and pharmaceutical sectors are experiencing notable shifts, with Eli Lilly acquiring psychedelics-focused AtaiBeckley for $2.8 billion to expand its neuroscience pipeline, Merck receiving FDA approval for an oral PCSK9 cholesterol drug, and prediction markets now entering biopharma through Kalshi's new offerings on clinical trial outcomes and regulatory approvals. Meanwhile, breakthrough neurotechnology is advancing—a brain-computer interface successfully restored sensation and movement in a quadriplegic patient—while digital health companies like TytoCare continue raising significant capital and pivoting their leadership structures. Administrative and governance challenges are also surfacing, including internal censorship at the American Diabetes Association journal, division among medical experts over Defense Department testosterone screening mandates, and ongoing questions about private equity's impact on independent medicine. Finally, emerging technologies from \"headless AI\" systems targeting healthcare bottlenecks to virtual reality applications in mental health are expanding the digital health toolkit, even as new risks like cognitive spoofing and fake AI expertise demand scrutiny.",
